  "A rigorous ʿilal (hidden-defect) examination separates what is certainly prophetic from what entered from companion/Kaʿb eschatology: under the classical majority method (al-Bukhārī, Muslim, al-Dāraquṭnī's main text), the sentence is graded ṣaḥīḥ marfūʿ. Under strict ʿilal criticism, however, strong evidence indicates that only 'between the two blasts are forty' is securely prophetic, while the bone clause was originally a statement of Abū Hurayra from the eschatological lore of his circle (Kaʿb al-Aḥbār / Ḥimṣ) whose attribution to the Prophet ﷺ occurred through transmission conflation (wahm / idrāj). This hits the al-Aʿmash family most strongly, Hammām moderately, and Mālik ← al-Aʿraj hardly at all—so 'ḍaʿīf' is not classically proven, though attribution to Abū Hurayra is the better-explained original form.",
