const M99=AV('100-und-99-barmherzigkeit','1284302444712693865');
card('islam/creed/attributes/mercy-100-and-99',{
 title:"The 100 mercies: Divine Attribute vs. created effects",
 k:"Creed · Ibn Taymiyyah · Bukhārī & Muslim",
 blurb:"Why 'Allah created mercy in a hundred parts' refers to created mercies and blessings—leaving Allah's eternal Attribute of Mercy uncreated and indivisible.",
 claim:"The ḥadīth saying 'Allah created mercy in 100 parts, sent down 1 and kept 99' implies that Allah's Attribute of Mercy is created and divisible into parts.",
 tree:N(M99,
  "If Allah 'created' (khalaqa) mercy in 100 parts, then either Allah's Attribute of Mercy is created, or His essence/attribute is divisible into 100 pieces.",
  "Ibn Taymiyyah resolves this cleanly through the ontological distinction between Allah's eternal Attributes (ṣifāt) and His created effects (makhlūqāt / āthār). Because the ḥadīth explicitly states 'Allah created (khalaqa) mercy,' and whatever is created cannot be eternal while Allah's Attribute of Mercy is eternal and uncreated, the word 'mercy' (raḥma) here denotes created manifestations of mercy—not the divine Attribute itself.",
  "Ibn Taymiyyah's ontological & linguistic distinction",
  ["Hadith text (Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī & Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim): 'Allah created mercy in a hundred parts; He sent one part down to the earth and kept ninety-nine parts back with Him.'",
   "Linguistic proof (khalaqa): The ḥadīth explicitly uses the verb 'created' (khalaqa). Logically: (1) what is created is necessarily non-eternal; (2) Allah's Attribute of Mercy is eternal and uncreated; (3) therefore 'mercy' in this ḥadīth cannot refer to the divine Attribute, but to a created reality.",
   "Metaphysical differentiation — Mercy as Attribute (Ṣifa): Allah's Attribute of Mercy is eternal, uncreated, indivisible, and subsistent in His essence. Real division into 100 parts would entail composition, divisibility, and contingency—which is theologically impossible.",
   "Metaphysical differentiation — Mercy as Created Effect (Athar / ʿAyn): The 100 mercies are created blessings, created capacities, and concrete manifestations of compassion, provision, and forgiveness. The 1 part on earth encompasses human sympathy, maternal care, compassion among animals ('so that even the beast lifts its hoof from its young'—a created effect in hearts, not Allah's essence), while the 99 parts kept with Him are eschatological mercies: forgiveness on Judgment Day, ranks of Paradise, and salvation from punishment."],
  ["Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī (100 mercies)","Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim (100 mercies)","Ibn Taymiyyah on created vs. uncreated raḥma"],
  [
   N(M99,
    "Why does the ḥadīth call created gifts 'mercy' if mercy is a Divine Attribute?",
    "Ibn Taymiyyah notes this is standard Arabic metonymy—naming the effect after its cause, just as Paradise itself is called 'My Mercy' in other authentic narrations—while the 1% vs. 99% ratio serves a profound spiritual purpose.",
    "Semantic metonymy & spiritual function (teleology)",
    ["Semantic precision (metonymy): The term 'mercy' (raḥma) is used metonymically here, naming the effect after its cause—just as Paradise is called 'My Mercy' in other traditions. Thus no divine Attribute is created; rather, a created gift of grace is named after the Attribute that bestows it.",
     "Teleological & spiritual function (Rajāʾ and Khawf): Ibn Taymiyyah reads the ḥadīth both ontologically and spiritually as instruction in hope (rajāʾ), reverent fear (khawf), detachment from the world, and priority of the Hereafter.",
     "Finitude of this world vs. vastness of the Hereafter: All compassion witnessed across human and animal history is only 1% of Allah's created mercy—showing how small the dunyā is compared to the Ākhirah, where 99 parts await manifestation, grounding hope in forgiveness and refuting despair.",
     "Systematic synthesis (three levels): (1) Ontological—the 100 mercies are created effects, not Allah's essence; (2) Theological—Allah's Attributes remain eternal, indivisible, and uncreated; (3) Teleological—the ḥadīth orients the believer toward the Hereafter, preserving both divine transcendence and the literal wording of the ḥadīth."],
    ["Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī","Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim","Ibn Taymiyyah (ontological & semantic analysis)"],
    [],
    {visual:{kind:'steps',items:[
       ["Ontological Level","The 100 mercies are created effects (makhlūqāt / āthār), not Allah's essence."],
       ["Theological Level","Allah's Attribute (ṣifa) of Mercy remains eternal, indivisible, and uncreated."],
       ["Semantic Level","'Raḥma' is metonymy (effect named after cause), like calling Paradise 'My Mercy.'"],
       ["Teleological Level","1% worldly compassion vs. 99% eschatological grace instills hope (rajāʾ) and perspective."]
     ]}})
  ],
  {facts:[["Sources","Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī & Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim"],["Key Verb","'khalaqa' (created) → created effect"],["1 Part","All compassion in the dunyā"],["99 Parts","Eschatological forgiveness & Paradise"]],
   quotes:[
    {by:"Hadith of the 100 Mercies (sense summary)",en:"Allah created mercy in a hundred parts. He sent one part down to the earth and kept ninety-nine parts back with Him... so that even the beast lifts its hoof from its young.",ref:"Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī & Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim"}
   ],
   visual:{kind:'compare',
    left:{title:"Mercy as Divine Attribute (Ṣifa)",items:["Eternal and uncreated","Indivisible (no composition or contingency)","Subsists in Allah's essence"]},
    right:{title:"The 100 Created Mercies (Āthār)",items:["Created blessings & capacities (khalaqa)","Quantitative division in creation (1 vs. 99)","1% worldly care; 99% Hereafter salvation"]}},
   shots:[
    S('pdf-mercy99-p1.jpg','Ibn Taymiyyah on the 100 Mercies — ontological thesis & linguistic proof from "khalaqa" (p. 1).'),
    S('pdf-mercy99-p2.jpg','Metaphysical distinction between eternal ṣifa and 100 created āthār (p. 2).')
   ]}),
 sum:"Because the ḥadīth explicitly says Allah 'created' (khalaqa) the 100 mercies, Ibn Taymiyyah explains them metonymically as created manifestations (1% in earthly compassion, 99% in the Hereafter)—leaving Allah's eternal Attribute of Mercy uncreated and indivisible."});

